1.) File system space management
Pseudo code:
BEGIN
SET threshold = 80%
usage = GET_DISK_USAGE()
IF usage > threshold THEN
    PRINT "Disk usage is high"
    log_files = GET_LOG_FILES()
    SORT log_files BY age ASCENDING
    FOR each file IN log_files DO
        IF file IS old THEN
            DELETE file
        ELSE
            COMPRESS file
        ENDIF
        usage = GET_DISK_USAGE()
        IF usage <= threshold THEN
            BREAK
        ENDIF
    ENDFOR
    IF usage > threshold THEN
        PRINT "Warning: Disk usage is still high"
    ELSE
        PRINT "Disk usage reduced successfully"
    ENDIF
ELSE
    PRINT "Disk usage is normal"
ENDIF
END

Explanation:
The algorithm continuously checks disk usage. If usage exceeds 80%, it identifies log files and deletes old files or compresses them. It repeatedly checks the disk and stops when usage falls below 80%.

2.)Priority-based CPU scheduling
Pseudo code:
BEGIN
INPUT process_list
SORT process_list BY priority DESCENDING
WHILE process_list IS NOT EMPTY DO
    SELECT process WITH highest priority
    ALLOCATE CPU TIME
    EXECUTE process
    IF process IS completed THEN
        REMOVE process FROM process_list
        PRINT "Process completed"
    ELSE
        REDUCE priority slightly
        ADD process BACK TO process_list
    ENDIF
    SORT process_list BY priority DESCENDING
ENDWHILE
PRINT "All processes completed"
END

Explanation:
The algorithm sorts processes according to their priority. The process with the highest priority gets the CPU first. If it is not completed, it is placed back into the queue with a slightly reduced priority.

3.)RAM management for multiple virtual machines
Pseudo code:
BEGIN
SET total_RAM = 16 GB
SET host_reserved = 4 GB
SET min_RAM = 2 GB
available_RAM = total_RAM - host_reserved
INPUT number_of_VMs
FOR i = 1 TO number_of_VMs DO
    IF available_RAM >= min_RAM THEN
        ALLOCATE min_RAM TO VM[i]
        available_RAM = available_RAM - min_RAM
        PRINT "RAM allocated to VM", i
    ELSE
        PRINT "Insufficient RAM for VM", i
    ENDIF
ENDFOR
PRINT "Remaining RAM =", available_RAM
END

Explanation:
The algorithm first reserves 4 GB for the host system. The remaining RAM is distributed among VMs, with each VM receiving at least 2 GB. If there is not enough RAM, the VM is not allocated memory.

4.)Hypervisor selection
Pseudo code:
BEGIN
INPUT performance_required
INPUT budget
INPUT hardware_access
IF performance_required = HIGH
   AND hardware_access = TRUE THEN
    selected_hypervisor = "Type 1 Hypervisor"
ELSE IF budget = LOW
   AND performance_required != HIGH THEN
    selected_hypervisor = "Type 2 Hypervisor"
ELSE IF hardware_access = TRUE THEN
    selected_hypervisor = "Type 1 Hypervisor"
ELSE
    selected_hypervisor = "Type 2 Hypervisor"
ENDIF
PRINT "Selected Hypervisor =", selected_hypervisor
END

Explanation:
The algorithm selects a Type 1 hypervisor when high performance and direct hardware access are required. A Type 2 hypervisor is selected when the budget is low or direct hardware access is unavailable.

5.)CPU and memory resource limiting
Pseudo code:
BEGIN
INPUT application_list
FOR each app IN application_list DO
    CREATE cgroup FOR app
    SET CPU_LIMIT = predefined CPU limit
    SET MEMORY_LIMIT = predefined memory limit
    APPLY CPU_LIMIT TO app
    APPLY MEMORY_LIMIT TO app
    ASSIGN app PROCESS TO cgroup
ENDFOR
PRINT "CPU and memory limits applied successfully"
END

Explanation:
The algorithm creates a cgroup for each application and assigns CPU and memory limits. This prevents one application from consuming excessive resources and affecting other applications.
